WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.270
- [Instructor] Person A here made $100,000

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.640
in the year that we care about,

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.920
and he's getting ready to pay his taxes,

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.580
and he's got some deductions.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.270
He made a $2,000 donation to charity

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:14.610
and a $5,000 donation to state taxes,

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.220
or not a donation, (chuckles)
he paid $5,000 in state taxes.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.400
So his total deductions right over here,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.310
and we would call these
his itemized deductions,

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.700
'cause he has literally put

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.440
all of the items that he's deducting,

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.520
so his itemized,

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.400
itemized deductions

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.310
are going to be $7,000.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.840
Now, before he just decides
to subtract that $7,000

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.200
from his income to get
his new taxable income,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.070
he should compare that against
the standard deduction.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:44.270
No matter what you do here,

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:47.280
the IRS will give you
just a freebie deduction.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.600
And if you're a single person,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.920
and this is, obviously,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.290
depending on what year you're filing in,

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.950
but at the time of this video,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.550
the standard deduction,

00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:00.550
the standard deduction is
$6,100 for a single person.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.590
So in this person's situation,

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.020
his itemized deductions

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.520
were larger than his standard deductions,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.380
so this is what he is going to take.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.760
He can't take both of these,

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.190
so he can't deduct 13,100.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.600
He would wanna pick the
larger of these two things.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.600
So his taxable income will
be $100,000 minus $7,000.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.530
So his taxable income

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.684
is going to be 93,

00:01:29.684 --> 00:01:32.020
$93,000.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.440
This is what he's going to pay taxes on.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.170
Now let's think about
Couple B right over here.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.520
They, as a couple, made $100,000.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.340
They're married, they're filing jointly.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.330
And they've made a $4,000
donation to charity.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.090
They've paid $5,000 in state taxes.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.860
And they've paid $3,000
in mortgage interest.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.170
And all of these are tax deductions.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.220
So what are their total
itemized deductions?

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.950
So, itemized deductions.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.490
Let's see, four plus five is nine,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:07.110
plus three is $12,000
in itemized deductions.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.250
Now, you might say, oh, this is great.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.930
They can deduct 12,000 from their 100,000.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.580
But once again, we wanna compare it

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.010
against the standard deduction.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.980
It's not gonna be the
same standard deduction

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.420
as what we saw for a single person.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.560
Now, they're married filing jointly.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.990
It's actually twice as large.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.730
Their standard deduction,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:28.547
their standard deduction
is going to be 12,200.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.150
So even though they made
all of these donations

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.420
or they paid these taxes

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.150
and they paid this mortgage interest,

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.660
it still didn't become larger

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:37.830
than their standard deductions,

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.470
so it's still in their best interest

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.840
to just go ahead with
their standard deduction.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.450
So it really didn't matter,
from a tax point of view,

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.850
whether or not they made these donations.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.130
'Cause they still didn't get past

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.150
this threshold right over here.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.030
Once again, you have to pick
the larger of these two.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.110
You don't get both of these.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.100
So their taxable income,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.290
Couple B's taxable
income, in this scenario,

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.970
is going to be 100,000 minus 12,200,

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:04.803
which is what?

00:03:04.803 --> 00:03:07.420
100,000 minus 12,000 would be 88,000.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.420
Minus another 200 would be
$87,800 of taxable income.

